{
  "case_id": "2022-21-908",
  "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Bankruptcy law gives honest but unfortunate debtors a fresh start by extinguishing all their debts. Exceptions are narrow, must be clearly expressed, and reflect debtors' intentional wrongs, not someone else's."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respondent and the government argue that anyone's fraud counts so long as the debtor winds up with a fraud-related debt."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Ms. Harris, the 523(a)(2)(A) does not focus on the debtor at least textually. It focus -- focuses on the debt. And it is in the passive voice, but it's talking about money or debt that's obtained by fraud. How do you convert that into a statute that is focusing on the debtor?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Well, you seem to be saying, Ms. Harris, that Congress is just careless when it writes this statute. And that may be true. There are some statutes where Congress is careless."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: So, Ms. Harris, even assuming that we agree about your reading that it has to be the debtor's fraud, I -- I don't understand how you get away from principles of vicarious liability."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: So, under common law, aren't you liable for the fraud of an agent or partner?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: -- if --if they were partners and the form she signed said, I didn't commit any fraud, she -- she signed a statement to the bank that said, I'm making all true statements, not saying my husband is, but I'm making all true statements, she is not liable then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: You keep bouncing back and forth on this, and I -- I want to understand your position clearly. Is it your position that any debt obtained by fraud is non-dischargeable, or is it your view that any debt obtained by fraud in connection with a partnership or agency relationship of the debtor is what is non-dischargeable?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: But, counsel, with respect to the text of this one, I guess I'm trying to figure out why the way in which you're interpreting it is the -- is the most narrow way."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: If I say John's property -- John's house was obtained by fraud, what do you understand that to mean?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Well, if I just say that, aren't you going to understand me to be saying that it was John who obtained the house by fraud? Not that somebody who obtained the house in 1885, when Strang was decided, obtained it by fraud, and after that time, it's had a whole chain of owners, and John is only the latest one?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: But -- but your rule is even if you don't know. And, you know, this idea of once there's liability the statute gets out of the way, well, there's always going to be liability because there wouldn't be a debt unless there had been liability."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0003-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The plain text of Section 523(a)(2)(A) bars discharge of Petitioner's debt to Respondent. That debt is, in the words of the statute, \"a debt for money obtained by actual fraud.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-908",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4c259655120889cc82ee1762df2690c679edac8f713216be86e3ab295882e7f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4c2596551208-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Ms. Ross, could you just comment? You heard the various hypotheticals about trying to find a limit to your -- to Respondent's theory that you apparently share. Would you spend a few minutes on that, what the limit is?"
    },
    {
